# MEASUREMENT PROBE — the `Admit::NatRed` population, split by head constant
# and operand shape. Worktree-only, never to be landed.
#
# `git apply scripts/nat-red-census.diff` onto a scratch worktree, then
#
#   cargo build --release --features tg,stepcount --bin nanoda_bin
#   ./target/release/nanoda_bin cfg.json                 # the census as it stands
#   NANODA_NAT_RULED=1 ./target/release/nanoda_bin …     # with link 3 switched on
#
# `cfg.json` is `good/init-prelude.ndjson`, `nat_extension` and
# `string_extension` on, `num_threads: 1`, `unsafe_permit_all_axioms`.
#
# WHY IT EXISTS. `derived::nat::ble_transfer` serves exactly one shape —
# `Nat.ble` at two numerals — and a deletion of the `nat_reduce` row prices the
# whole row rather than that shape. This is the census *inside the rule*: how
# much of the population a construction can reach, asked before it is built.
# The answer on `init-prelude` (2026-08-11) is 466 of 503 with link 3 on, all at
# operands ≤ 12; the other 37 are `Nat.beq`, `Nat.pow` and `Nat.succ` at
# non-numeral arguments, which this bridge does not serve at all.
#
# What it adds: a `BTreeMap` census keyed `<head> <operand shape> <magnitude>`,
# filled at `TypeChecker::admit` when the site is `Admit::NatRed`, and printed
# after `inst_report`. Nothing is decided by it; the ledger is byte-identical
# with it applied.
